import{_ as o,o as t,c as a,an as d}from"./chunks/framework.BuqOfud7.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Model protocol","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"protocol.md","filePath":"protocol.md","lastUpdated":null}'),i={name:"protocol.md"};function n(s,e,l,r,c,h){return t(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[d('<h1 id="Model-protocol" tabindex="-1">Model protocol <a class="header-anchor" href="#Model-protocol" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Model protocol {#Model-protocol}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A model is any Julia value that supplies a conditional observation likelihood. NLMEEstimation supplies the subject-level random-effect prior, <code>ηᵢ ~ N(0, Ω)</code>, and represents Ω by blockwise log-Cholesky coordinates. The model supplies θ and σ in its own unconstrained coordinates.</p><p>Every model implements <code>nsubjects(model)</code> and <code>nlme_layout(model)</code>. It can use either evaluation style:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Style</th><th style="text-align:right;">Required conditional likelihood</th><th style="text-align:right;">How estimators evaluate it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>PerSubjectEvaluation()</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>conditional_loglikelihood(model, i, θ, σ, η)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">The package gathers subject calls into a batch.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>BatchedEvaluation()</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>conditional_loglikelihoods(model, subjects, θ, σ, H)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">One call evaluates the listed subjects; column <code>k</code> of <code>H</code> is that subject&#39;s η.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The likelihood excludes the η prior. Derivatives may be provided directly or obtained through <code>ad_backend(model)</code> with DifferentiationInterface. Reverse-mode Enzyme is the tested backend. Per-subject η Hessian blocks are optional; set <code>hessian_ad_backend(model) = nothing</code> for a gradient-only model. <a href="./requirements">PPL and AD requirements</a> lists which derivatives each estimator uses; every method runs on first-order AD.</p><p>A model rejects a point outside its domain by returning <code>-Inf</code>, or by throwing a <code>DomainError</code>, <code>OverflowError</code> or <code>PosDefException</code> as a Stan-semantics model does (a matrix exponential of an overflowed rate, the Cholesky factor of an indefinite matrix). At a trial point the estimators treat both alike. <code>focei</code>&#39;s search scores the evaluation as a very large objective. SAEM&#39;s MH and MALA kernels reject that subject&#39;s proposal; when a batched evaluation throws, they evaluate the subjects one at a time to find which ones. SAEM&#39;s numerical and MAP M-steps halve their line-search step. The point a search starts from must evaluate finitely.</p><p>FOCE and FOCEI require <code>observation_model(model) = GaussianObservations()</code> and observation means, variances, and first η Jacobians. SAEM can use <code>mu_referencing(model)</code> for a closed-form update of the corresponding fixed effects and Ω; remaining population coordinates use a numerical M-step.</p><p>A batched model may keep its batched likelihood and η derivatives while using subject-level population gradients in SAEM. Implement <code>conditional_loglikelihood_and_population_gradient(model, i, θ, σ, η)</code> and declare <code>saem_population_gradient_style(model) = PerSubjectEvaluation()</code>. SAEM then sums those subject values and gradients for the numerical M-step. This choice does not change <code>evaluation_style(model)</code> or any other estimator step. For different routes in the two numerical M-step modes, define <code>saem_population_gradient_style(model, ::Val{:online})</code> or <code>saem_population_gradient_style(model, ::Val{:mixture})</code>. A missing mode-specific method uses the one-argument style. This lets a model use compact subject-cell gradients for the bounded-memory online update and a batched gradient for the retained-draw mixture.</p><p>A model can hold a random-effect covariance family, or part of one, at a known value by declaring <code>fixed_omega(model)</code>. It returns <code>family =&gt; FixedOmega(…)</code> pairs, where a family is an <code>eta_blocks</code> entry of <code>NLMELayout</code> (or a <code>family_sizes</code> entry of <code>RaggedNLMELayout</code>) by position. <code>FixedOmega(Ω)</code> fixes the whole family, matching nlmixr2&#39;s <code>fix()</code> on an ω block and NONMEM&#39;s <code>OMEGA … FIX</code>. <code>FixedOmega(sd = [0.3, nothing], correlation = nothing)</code> fixes the listed standard deviations, and <code>FixedOmega(correlation = R)</code> fixes the correlation, in <code>Ω = D R D</code> terms. FOCE(I) searches only the free Ω coordinates. SAEM leaves a fully fixed family unchanged and maximizes the expected Gaussian random-effect density over a partially fixed family&#39;s free standard deviations and correlation. Both report the fixed values in <code>omega</code> and <code>omega_matrix</code>. Their starting <code>ω0</code> must hold the fixed values; <code>apply_fixed_omega(model, ω)</code> moves a vector onto them. <code>empirical_bayes</code> evaluates at the <code>ω</code> it is given.</p><p>Run <code>check_protocol(model, θ, σ, ω)</code> while implementing a model. It checks dimensions, agreement between evaluation styles, subject separability, available derivatives, declared observation and mu-referencing traits, and that <code>ω</code> holds any declared <code>fixed_omega</code> values.</p><p>The <a href="./pk-comparison">PK comparison</a> shows a complete batched BRM+RKPPL model and the calls used to fit it. The <a href="./api">API reference</a> lists every protocol entry point.</p>',11)])])}const u=o(i,[["render",n]]);export{p as __pageData,u as default};
